+        name: "compact_agent".to_string(),
+        description: "Ask a colleague to compact its own context: it keeps the thread, its identity and its understanding of the work, and drops the transcript weight behind them. Use this instead of replacing a colleague that has grown heavy — a new one starts light but has to rebuild its picture of the project before it can help, and that rebuild is the expensive part. Judge by the situation, not by arithmetic. It is usually worth compacting when a colleague has just finished something whole — shipped a PR, closed out a phase, handed back a conclusion — because that is when the detail behind it stops being needed. `list_agents` reports each colleague's `context_used_percent`; treat it as one input among several, weighted by what the colleague is in the middle of. Around 60% it is worth doing at the next natural break. Around 75% it is worth doing soon even mid-task, because every later step is being charged at that size. Past 85% the runtime will compact on its own shortly, which costs more than choosing the moment yourself. A colleague well under half full, or one partway through something it cannot easily resume, is usually better left alone. Compacting costs one summarizing turn and a few seconds; the colleague continues from where it was.".to_string(),
